- remove or bypass the restrictions in this licence.

Any of those activities requires separate written permission from the Licensor unless another applicable licence independently grants the required permission for the particular material concerned.

## 4. Studying the Source

You may inspect and study the Source.

You may use knowledge gained from studying the Work, including general ideas, programming techniques, algorithms, concepts, and functionality, where copyright law does not restrict their use.

This permission does not allow You to copy, translate, reconstruct, adapt, or reproduce a substantial part of the protected implementation of the Work in another project.

The same rule applies regardless of how that reproduction is performed, including:

- manual rewriting;
- copying and editing;
- translation to another programming language;
- automated conversion;
- code generation; or
- use of artificial intelligence.

An independently written implementation is not prohibited merely because it performs the same function or uses the same unprotected ideas.

## 5. Mods, Plugins and Compatible Software

Software does not become subject to CleanroomMC License merely because it interacts with the Work.

You may create and distribute independent:

- mods;
- plugins;
- extensions;
- integrations;
- runtime patches;
- Java agents;
- Mixins;
- ASM or other bytecode transformations; and
- other compatible software,

provided that the distributed software does not contain or redistribute a substantial portion of protected code or other protected material governed by this licence.

Using an API, hook, event, protocol, file format, extension point, or other interface provided by the Work does not by itself make another program a Modification of the Work.

Use of reflection, Mixins, ASM, instrumentation, bytecode transformation, or similar techniques also does not by itself make another program a Modification.

Calling something a mod, plugin, patch, transformer, agent, or extension does not avoid this licence where that software actually contains or redistributes substantial protected material from the Work.

A Java member being declared `public` does not by itself mean that CleanroomMC guarantees that member as a supported public API.

Nothing in this section changes the licence governing a separately licensed component under Section 2.

## 6. Private Distribution

You may distribute an unmodified copy of the Work through Private Distribution.

When doing so, You must:

1. keep access limited to the intended private recipients;
2. include this licence or a clear reference to CleanroomMC License Version 1.0;
3. retain existing copyright, licence, patent, attribution, and trademark notices; and
4. not knowingly allow recipients to publicly redistribute the Work in violation of this licence.

Private Distribution does not give You permission to distribute a Modification unless that Modification is separately authorised by the Licensor.

Where a privately distributed package contains separately licensed components, those components remain subject to their respective licences.

## 7. Public Distribution

You may not publicly distribute the Work without prior written permission from the Licensor.

You may not publicly distribute a Modification of the Work without prior written permission from the Licensor.

This prohibition applies to both Source and Binary forms.

For clarity, without written permission You may not publish:

- a public fork;
- a public mirror;
- a modified build;
- a repackaged build;
- the Work as part of another publicly downloadable project; or
- a substantial portion of the Work extracted into another publicly distributed project.

The fact that the Source is publicly visible does not give You permission to redistribute it.

This section applies only to material governed by CleanroomMC License.

It does not prohibit Distribution of separately licensed material where that material's applicable licence permits such Distribution.

## 8. Unauthorised Disclosure

A person does not automatically violate this licence merely because a third party obtains an unauthorised copy from a private location.

You violate this licence if You intentionally publish the Work contrary to this licence or knowingly assist another person in doing so.

If a copy under Your control is unintentionally exposed, You must take practical steps to remove or restrict that exposure after becoming aware of it.

A third party's unauthorised conduct does not by itself terminate Your rights where You did not intentionally assist or enable that conduct.

